Package: tufter
Type: Package
Title: Implement Edward Tufte's Principles of Graphical Design
Version: 0.5.2
Authors@R: person("Charles", "Crabtree",
                  email = "charles.crabtree@monash.edu",
                  role = c("aut", "cre"),
                  comment = c(ORCID = "0000-0001-5144-8671"))
Description: A toolkit that turns Edward Tufte's principles of statistical
    graphics into working 'ggplot2' code, and into measurements that can be
    applied to a figure you have already drawn. The drawing side supplies the
    graphical forms Tufte designed or argued for: range frames and quartile
    frames, minimal box plots, dot-dash marginal distributions, bar charts with
    the gridlines erased through the bars, Cleveland dot plots, sparklines,
    slopegraphs, small multiples, muted palettes, and direct labelling in place
    of legends. The measuring side supplies the quantities Tufte defined: the
    data-ink ratio, estimated empirically by rendering a plot with and without
    its data layers, the lie factor, and data density. 'tufte_audit()' runs
    both sides together. It grades the principles for which Tufte states a
    criterion a graphic either meets or does not, and merely measures those for
    which he states only a direction, so that no threshold of the package
    author's is presented in his voice. For the underlying principles see Tufte
    (2001) "The Visual Display of Quantitative Information" (ISBN:0961392142),
    Tufte (1990) "Envisioning Information" (ISBN:0961392118), Tufte (1997)
    "Visual Explanations" (ISBN:0961392126), and Tufte (2006) "Beautiful
    Evidence" (ISBN:0961392177). For banking to 45 degrees see Cleveland,
    McGill and McGill (1988) <doi:10.1080/01621459.1988.10478598>.
